# Lecture notes for Module 5 (one entry per PDF page of module5.pdf).
# Voice: teacher speaking to students, simple language.
# Target: at most 2 minutes per slide (about 140-175 words at ~135 words per minute).

NOTES = [
("Title: Legal, Ethical and Policy Issues",
"Good morning, everyone. Today we begin Module 5, Legal, Ethical and Policy Issues. So far in this course, we have mostly talked about technology: ciphers, attacks, viruses and firewalls. Today we look at a different side of security: the law and our values. Look at the picture on the right. It is a balance scale, the symbol of justice. On one side there is a key, and on the other side there is a lock. This picture captures today's main question. How do we balance the needs of security and law enforcement with the rights and privacy of ordinary people? This module has two lessons. Lesson 5 is Key Escrow, which is about governments wanting a way to read encrypted messages. Lesson 8 is Legal Issues, which covers ethics, privacy, digital signatures, Indian cyber laws, government initiatives, patents and export controls. Some parts of this module are about real Indian laws, so they are very useful for your future careers too."),

("Roadmap of This Module",
"Let me show you our road map. On the left are four topics from Lesson 5, Key Escrow. First, the escrow dilemma: lawful wiretaps versus privacy. Second, the Clipper chip and the Escrowed Encryption Standard, where keys are split and stored with escrow agencies. Third, trustee-based escrow, where a private key is split among many trustees. Fourth, the politics of key escrow: who gains and who loses. On the right are four topics from Lesson 8, Legal Issues. Fifth, ethics and law: privacy, a set of four ethical categories, and digital signatures. Sixth, Indian cyber law, mainly the IT Act 2000 and its important sections. Seventh, other Indian Acts: the Contract Act, the Indian Penal Code, the Copyright Act, the Consumer Protection Act and the Specific Relief Act. Eighth, government initiatives, patents and export controls. The first half is about one big debate. The second half is about the actual laws that apply to us."),

("Lesson 5: Key Escrow",
"We now begin Lesson 5, Key Escrow. Let me start with a simple word: escrow. In daily life, escrow means something valuable is kept with a trusted third person, and it is released only when a certain condition is met. For example, when you buy a house, the money may be kept with a lawyer and given to the seller only after the papers are signed. Now apply this to cryptography. You encrypt your messages with a key. Key escrow means a copy of your key is kept with trusted agents, and given to the police only if a court orders it. This sounds reasonable at first. But it raises a very difficult question. Should the government be able to read our private messages? In this lesson, we will see how key escrow works technically, and then we will discuss the politics, the arguments for and against. Please think about your own opinion as we go."),

("The Dilemma: Lawful Wiretaps vs. Privacy",
"Look at the balance scale on the left. On one side is law and national security. On the other side is individual privacy. This is the dilemma, which means a difficult choice. Read the points on the right. Court-authorized line tapping, which means listening to phone calls with a court's permission, helps bring criminals to justice. It also stops people from using networks for unlawful purposes. Now, strong public-key cryptography means nobody can read your messages without your key. That is great for honest people, but it can also be a big help to criminals and terrorists. So many bills, which are proposed laws, have suggested that a government agency should be able to get the plain text of any communication, if the law allows it. Now read the blue box. The challenge is to build a cryptosystem that protects individual privacy, but still allows wiretaps that a court has approved. That is the goal of key escrow."),

("Two Unpopular Options",
"Without key escrow, how could the government read encrypted messages? Look at the two options in the tree. Option one, in orange, is to use weak crypto systems. These are systems that the authorities can break with moderate effort. But look at the box below it. The problem is that if the police can break it, so can everybody else, including criminals and hackers. So weak crypto makes everyone less safe. Option two, in red, is surrendering your secret key. This means handing your key to the authority in advance, before any crime is even suspected. Imagine being asked to give a copy of your house key to the police, just in case. Most people would feel very uncomfortable. Now read the red box. Both options alarmed many citizens. They felt that privacy should come before national security and law enforcement. Key escrow was proposed as a middle path between these two bad options."),

("What Is Key Escrow?",
"Let us see the idea of key escrow in a picture. On the left is a user. In the middle is a key. Below the key is a box labelled escrow agent. A copy of the decryption key, or pieces of it, is kept safely with the escrow agent. At the bottom is law enforcement, the police. Notice the arrow from the escrow agent to the police says court order. The key is released only when a court orders it. Read the blue box. That is the whole idea. Now read the points below. Key escrow was the heart of the United States government's Clipper program. It was formalized as the Escrowed Encryption Standard, or EES. And the word escrow simply means held by a third party until some condition is met. So in theory, your messages stay private, and only in a real criminal case, with a court's permission, can the police read them. In the next slides we see how this was done technically."),

("The EES Chip and Its Split Key",
"Let us look at how the Escrowed Encryption Standard worked. Look at the black chip on the left. It is a tamperproof chip, which means if anyone tries to open it or change it, it stops working or destroys its secrets. Each chip has two things inside: a unique ID number, shown here as 4F72, and a secret key, called U. Now look at the arrows. The secret key U is split into two pieces, U1 and U2. Piece U1 is stored, along with the chip's ID number, by escrow agency one. Piece U2 is stored by escrow agency two. Look at the formula on the right: U equals U1 XOR U2. You need both pieces to rebuild the key. Read the last bullet. Neither piece alone reveals anything about the key. So even if one agency is dishonest, or one agency is robbed, your key is still safe. Both agencies must cooperate to recover it."),

("How an EES Chip Sends Data",
"Now let us see what the chip does every time it sends data. Follow the diagram. On the left is the data file and a session key, called S. A session key is a temporary key used only for this one conversation. First, the data file is encrypted with the session key S. Second, the session key S itself is encrypted with the chip's secret key U. Now look at the packet on the right that is sent over the channel. It has three parts: the encrypted session key, the chip's ID number, and the encrypted traffic. Read the blue box. Every time the chip encrypts, it first encrypts the session key with its unique secret key, and then sends this encrypted session key and its ID number along with the encrypted message. Why send the ID? So that, if a court orders a wiretap, the police know which chip this message came from, and therefore which pieces to ask the escrow agencies for."),

("How Law Enforcement Decrypts",
"Now let us follow the police when a court has approved a wiretap. Look at the five numbered circles. Step one: listen to the traffic and read the chip's ID number from the packet. Step two: take the court order to both escrow agencies and collect the two pieces, U1 and U2, for that ID. Step three: XOR the two pieces together to rebuild the chip's secret key U. Step four: use U to decrypt the session key S. Step five: use S to decrypt the actual message traffic. Look at the balance scale at the top. All of this happens only with a court order. Now read the green box. Without the court-ordered help of both escrow agencies, the traffic stays private. But with it, a lawful wiretap becomes possible. So the design tries to give us both privacy and law enforcement at the same time. Whether it really succeeds is what we will discuss later."),

("Secret Splitting with XOR",
"Let me show you why splitting a key with XOR is so safe. Look at the bits on the left. The first row is piece U1. The second row is piece U2. The third row is the real key U. Each bit of U is the XOR of the two bits above it. Read the XOR rule on the right. If the two bits are the same, the result is zero. If they are different, the result is one. Let us check the first column together. One and zero are different, so the key bit is one. The second column: zero and one are different, so one. The third column: one and one are the same, so zero. Now the most important point. If you look at U1 alone, it looks completely random. U2 alone also looks completely random. Only when you combine them do you get the real key. Read the blue box. One agency, or a thief who robs one agency, learns nothing about the key."),

("Escrow with n Trustees",
"The study material also describes a more general escrow scheme, using n trustees instead of just two. Look at the diagram. Alice, on the left, has a private key. She breaks it into pieces, P1, P2, P3, P4, and gives one piece to each trustee. Read the points on the right. This works like a secret sharing scheme. Together, the authorities can rebuild the key. But there is a very clever extra property. Each trustee can verify that their piece is a valid piece of the private key, without rebuilding the whole key. Why is this important? It means Alice cannot cheat. She cannot send a trustee a random string of bits and hope to get away with it, because the trustee will check it. Now read the red box. On the other side, to steal Alice's key, an attacker called Mallory would have to corrupt all n trustees, not just one. The more trustees, the harder it is to abuse the system."),

("The Trustee Protocol",
"A protocol is simply a fixed set of steps that everyone follows. Let us go through the four steps of the trustee protocol. Step one: Alice creates her private and public key pair. She splits the private key into several public pieces and private pieces. Step two: Alice sends one public piece and its matching private piece to each trustee. These messages must be encrypted. She also sends her public key to the KDC. Look at the line at the bottom: KDC means Key Distribution Centre. Step three: each trustee independently does a calculation on its two pieces to check that they are correct. Then it stores the private piece safely, and sends the public piece to the KDC. Step four: the KDC does another calculation, using all the public pieces and Alice's public key. If everything is correct, the KDC signs Alice's public key, and either sends it back to her or publishes it in a database. Now Alice's key is officially registered."),

("When the Court Orders a Wiretap",
"What happens when a court orders a wiretap on Alice? Look at the diagram from left to right. On the left is the court order, shown by the balance scale. The order goes to all four trustees. Each trustee surrenders, which means gives up, its piece to the KDC, shown by the orange arrows. The KDC, in purple, rebuilds Alice's private key, shown by the red key on the right. Now the police can wiretap Alice's communications. Read the two boxes at the bottom carefully, because the contrast is important. The blue box says after the order: each trustee surrenders its piece, the KDC reconstructs the private key, and Alice can be wiretapped. The red box says before the order: neither the KDC nor any single trustee can rebuild the key. All the trustees are required. So the protection of Alice's privacy depends on the honesty of the trustees, and on the courts using this power fairly. Keep that in mind for the next slide."),

("The Politics: What Does the User Gain?",
"Now let us move from technology to politics. Look at the big question mark on the left. What are the advantages of key escrow for the ordinary user? Read the answer under it, from your study material: there really aren't any. Let us understand why, using the points on the right. Several companies also offered commercial key escrow, besides the government. But the user gains nothing that he could not do himself. He can already back up his own keys if he wants. What escrow guarantees is that the police can eavesdrop on his conversations and read his files, even though they are encrypted. It also guarantees that the NSA, an American intelligence agency, can eavesdrop on his international phone calls, without a warrant. The only small advantage is that he may be allowed to use cryptography in countries that would otherwise ban it. So from the user's point of view, key escrow mostly helps the government, not the user."),

("The Politics: Considerable Disadvantages",
"Key escrow also has considerable disadvantages. Look at the diagram. The user is in the middle, and around him are six things he must trust. He must trust the escrow agents' security procedures, so they do not lose or leak his key. He must trust the integrity, or honesty, of the people working there. He must trust the escrow agents not to change their policies. He must trust the government not to change its laws. He must trust those with lawful authority to use his keys lawfully and responsibly. And he must trust that the limits will hold even after a crisis. That last point is explained in the red box. Your study material asks us to imagine a major terrorist attack in a big city. After such an attack, people are angry and afraid. In that situation, what limits on the police might be thrown aside? Rules that seem strong today may be quickly removed tomorrow. That is the real risk of giving anyone a copy of your keys."),

("Where Escrow Leads: Legal Pressure",
"Let us think about where key escrow might lead. Follow the boxes from top to bottom. First, escrowed encryption is offered. Second, few people choose it voluntarily, because, as we saw, it gives them no advantage. Third, there is pressure to make it pay, because the government has spent money on it. Fourth, the obvious next step is a ban on non-escrowed encryption, which means making it illegal to use encryption without escrow. Read the points on the right. Your study material says it is hard to imagine escrow working without some legal pressure. A ban is probably the only way to force sophisticated criminals and terrorists to use it. Now read the red box. This raises many open questions. How hard would such a ban be to enforce? How would it affect cryptography as a subject of study? Would a researcher need a special licence just to study non-escrowed algorithms? These questions end Lesson 5. Next, we move to Lesson 8."),

("Lesson 8: Legal Issues",
"We now begin Lesson 8, Legal Issues. In Lesson 5 we discussed one big debate. In this lesson, we look more widely at ethics and law in computer security. Ethics means our sense of right and wrong. Law means the official rules made by the government, which can be enforced by courts. Something can be legal but unethical, and in some cases, something can be ethical but not yet covered by any law. This lesson has three parts. First, we will look at ethical issues, especially privacy, and at the legal status of digital signatures. Second, we will study the Indian laws that apply to information security: the IT Act 2000 and several other Acts. This part is very practical, because these laws apply to you and to the companies you will work for. Third, we will look at government initiatives in India, and at legal issues in cryptography such as patents and export controls."),

("The OSI Standard for Security Model",
"You already know the OSI model for networks, with its seven layers from physical to application. Your study material mentions a much less known standard on similar lines, called the OSI standard for Security Model. Look at the coloured stack on the left. It also defines seven layers, but these are layers of security. From the bottom: authentication, access control, non-repudiation, data integrity, confidentiality, assurance or availability, and notarization or signature. Notarization means an official confirmation that a document is genuine, like a notary stamping and signing a paper. Now read the blue box. These layers match the security services that we studied in Module 2: authentication, access control, integrity, confidentiality, availability and non-repudiation. So this is not new material, just a different way of organizing it. It is a good reminder that security, like networking, is built in layers, and each layer has its own job."),

("Ethical Issues: Privacy vs. the Greater Good",
"Many ethical and legal problems in computer security are about a balance between two things. On one side is the individual's right to privacy. On the other side is the greater good of a larger group, like a company or society. Look at the person in the middle, with a dashed circle around them. That circle is their personal space. Around it are real examples where privacy is under pressure. Companies track how employees use their computers. Cameras do crowd surveillance. Shops build customer profiles from what you buy. Your travel is tracked using your passport. And your phone's location is used to send you spam advertisements by SMS. Each of these may have a reason, like safety or business, but each also reduces privacy. Now read the blue box. To resolve such issues, the key question is: what is the person's expectation of privacy? For example, you expect privacy in your bedroom, but not on a public road."),

("Four Ethical Categories: PAPA",
"Your study material says ethical issues in security are classically divided into four categories. Here is an easy way to remember them: PAPA. Look at the four circles. P is for privacy. This is the right of a person to control their own personal information. A is for accuracy. This is about who is responsible for making sure information is authentic, faithful and correct. For example, if a bank records your balance wrongly, who is responsible? The second P is for property. This is about who owns the information, and who controls access to it. For example, who owns the photos you upload to a website? The second A is for accessibility. This is about what kind of information an organization has the right to collect, and how it will protect that information from unexpected problems. Please remember PAPA. If an examiner asks you to explain the ethical issues in computer security, these four words give you a perfect structure for your answer."),

("What Is Privacy?",
"Let us look more closely at privacy. Read the definition in the blue box. Privacy is the protection of personal or sensitive information. It is the desire to be left alone, as an extension of our personal space. Look at the picture on the left: a person inside circles, with the words left alone. Now read the three points. First, privacy may or may not be supported by local laws. In some countries it is strongly protected by law, and in others less so. Second, privacy is subjective. Subjective means it depends on the person. Different people have very different ideas about what is private. Some people share everything on social media. Others do not even share their phone number. Third, people trade different amounts of privacy for safety or convenience. For example, many of you happily share your location with a food delivery app, because it is convenient. That is a trade. The important thing is to make that trade knowingly, not by accident."),

("A Hierarchy of Regulatory Bodies",
"When we deal with legal issues, we must remember that rules come from different levels. Look at the four bars, from widest at the top to narrowest at the bottom. At the top is the international level. An example is the International Cyber Crime Treaty, an agreement between countries. Next is the federal, or national, level. Your study material gives American examples: FERPA for student records, GLB for banking, HIPAA for health records, DMCA for digital copyright, the Teach Act, the Patriot Act and Sarbanes-Oxley. In India, the IT Act 2000 is at this level. Next is the state level, with examples like UCITA and SB 1386. And at the bottom is the organization level, such as a company's computer use policy, or your college's lab rules. Read the blue box. These levels form a hierarchy. An organization's policy must obey state law. State law must obey national law. And national law must fit with international treaties. Lower rules cannot break higher ones."),

("Are Digital Signatures Real Signatures?",
"Here is an important legal question. You learned that a digital signature proves who sent a message. But will a court accept it as a real signature, like your handwritten signature? Follow the timeline. First, early legal research said that digital signatures would meet the requirements of the Uniform Commercial Code, or UCC, for legally binding signatures. Second, the General Accounting Office, or GAO, on a request from NIST, said digital signatures will meet the legal standards of handwritten signatures. Third, on the first of May 1995, the Utah Digital Signature Act came into effect. It gave a legal framework for using digital signatures in courts. Fourth, the American Bar Association, or ABA, which is an association of lawyers, produced a model act that states could use to make their own laws. Look at the line at the bottom for the full forms of these short names. So step by step, the legal world started accepting digital signatures."),

("The ABA Model Act and Its Limits",
"Let us look at the ABA model act in more detail. Read the blue box on the left. The model act tries to fit digital signatures into existing laws, such as the Uniform Commercial Code, rules of the US Federal Reserve, the common law of contracts and signatures, and United Nations conventions on the sale of goods and on bills of exchange. It also sets out the responsibilities and obligations of certification authorities, which are the trusted bodies that issue digital certificates. And it covers issues of liability, which means who pays if something goes wrong, along with limits and policies. Now read the red box. In the United States, laws about signatures and contracts are state laws, so the model act was written for states. The final goal is a single federal act. But here is the key limitation. At the time, the validity of digital signatures had not yet been tested in court. Look at the circles at the bottom. Legal precedent builds up slowly, case by case."),

("Until Courts Decide: The Paper Contract",
"So what should people do until the courts make clear decisions about digital signatures? Look at the diagram. Two people in the middle have a paper contract. It says: we agree to be bound by any document we digitally sign in future. Both of them sign this paper with normal handwritten signatures. Read the bullets. For digital signatures to carry the same authority as handwritten ones, they must first be used to sign a legally binding document, and then be challenged in court by one party. The court would then look at how secure the signature scheme is and give a ruling. Over time, as this happens again and again, a body of precedent builds up. Precedent means earlier court decisions that guide later ones. It would tell us which digital signature methods and key sizes are legally binding. This could take years. Until then, the study material recommends the simple trick shown here: first sign a paper contract agreeing to honour your future digital signatures."),

("Security and Law: Different Speeds",
"Look at the graph on the left. It shows change over time. The red curve is technology, which rises very fast. The blue line is law, which moves slowly in small steps. Read the bullets on the right. As computer security changes our lives, laws are made to encourage good behaviour and forbid bad behaviour, just like in any other part of society. But there is one big difference. Information technology moves much faster than the slow process of making laws. This causes three kinds of problems. Sometimes laws are too restrictive, which means too strict, for example rules about importing and exporting encryption, which limited business. Sometimes laws are too slow, for example digital signatures, where business waited for the law to catch up. And sometimes laws are both too fast and too slow, as with privacy laws. Now read the last line in italics. You will never satisfy everyone with a law, but a law does set the rules of the game, so everyone knows where they stand."),

("Regulations in India",
"Now let us come to India. Look at the diagram on the left. India has a unified judicial system, which means one single system of courts. At the top is the Supreme Court, then the High Courts, and then the Lower Courts. Read the points on the right. India has a detailed and well-defined legal system based on English common law. But the older Indian laws did not cover new things like the Internet and offshoring. Offshoring means foreign companies sending work to Indian companies. These created complex new legal issues. The Indian government responded proactively, which means it acted early. The first draft of the Information Technology Bill was introduced in 1999, and the IT Act came into force in 2000. Apart from the IT Act, the Copyright Act deals with copyright of computer programs. Now read the red box. At the time of your study material, India had no law made specifically for data protection. Instead, several proxy laws, meaning other laws, give indirect protection, as we will see next."),

("Information Technology Act, 2000",
"This is the most important Indian law for our subject: the Information Technology Act, 2000. Read the blue box at the bottom. In May 2000, both houses of the Indian Parliament passed the Information Technology Bill, which became the IT Act, 2000. It covers cyber laws and other laws related to information technology in India. Now look at the diagram. The IT Act is in the middle. The arrows point to four older laws that the IT Act amended, which means changed, to bring them up to date with the computer age. These are the Indian Penal Code of 1860, the Indian Evidence Act of 1872, the Bankers' Books Evidence Act of 1891, and the Reserve Bank of India Act of 1934. For example, the Evidence Act was changed so that electronic records could be accepted as evidence in court. In the next few slides, we will look at four important sections of the IT Act."),

("IT Act: Section 43",
"Let us start with Section 43 of the IT Act. Look at the diagram. On the left is a person without permission. The red arrows show four things this person does to a computer. Read the blue box. According to Section 43, if a person, without the permission of the person in charge of a computer system, does any of these things: accesses the system, downloads any data, introduces a virus, or causes denial of access, then that person is liable to pay a penalty of up to rupees 10 million, which is one crore rupees. Look at the orange box on the left showing the penalty. Notice two important things. First, the key words are without permission. If you have permission, it is not an offence. Second, the act does not have to be very serious. Simply downloading data or spreading a virus without permission can lead to this huge penalty. So please, never try to break into anyone's computer, even as a joke."),

("IT Act: Sections 65 and 66",
"Now two more sections. On the left is Section 65, about tampering with computer source code. Source code is the original program written by a programmer. Look at the small code example. Someone has commented out the line that checks the licence, so the software can be used illegally. Read the text. Section 65 says that anyone who knowingly or deliberately hides, destroys or alters any computer source code, or makes someone else do it, can be punished. On the right is Section 66, about hacking. Hacking is committed when someone, with the intention of causing wrongful loss or damage to the public or any person, destroys, deletes or alters information in a computer resource, reduces its value or usefulness, or harms it by any means. Now look at the punishment boxes. For both Section 65 and Section 66, the punishment is up to three years in jail, or a fine up to two lakh rupees, or both."),

("IT Act: Section 72",
"Section 72 is about breach of confidentiality and privacy. Look at the diagram. On the left is an official with lawful access. This person has a document, which they got access to legally, using powers given under the IT Act. On the right is an unauthorized person. The red dashed arrow says discloses. Read the blue box. If a person who has secured access to any electronic record, register, correspondence, information or document, under powers granted by the IT Act, discloses it to another person without the consent of the person concerned, they can be punished. Look at the punishment on the left: imprisonment up to two years, or a fine up to one lakh rupees, or both. Now read the red box carefully. This section does not apply to personal information disclosed by a website or by an email service provider. So it mainly covers officials and authorities who misuse information they got through their official powers."),

("IT Act Sections at a Glance",
"This table puts the four IT Act sections side by side. It is excellent for revision, so please copy it into your notebook. Let us read it row by row. Section 43: the offence is unauthorized access, downloading, introducing a virus or denial of access. The punishment is a penalty of up to rupees 10 million, or one crore. Section 65: the offence is tampering with computer source code. The punishment is up to three years and, or, a fine of two lakh rupees. Section 66: the offence is hacking. The punishment is again up to three years and, or, two lakh rupees. Section 72: the offence is breach of confidentiality and privacy. The punishment is up to two years and, or, one lakh rupees. Now read the exam tip at the bottom. Learn the section number, the offence and the punishment together, as one unit. Examiners often ask questions like: explain the IT Act 2000 with its important sections."),

("Indian Contract Act, 1872",
"Now let us look at other Indian laws that also help protect information. First, the Indian Contract Act of 1872. Read the blue box. A contract is a legally binding agreement between two or more persons. Look at the diagram for examples: buying goods, hiring a builder to construct a house, borrowing money, or ordering machinery from a manufacturer. All of these are contracts. The parties decide the terms, and they must then perform as agreed. Now read the red box. Breach of contract means violating the terms, or not performing your obligations. When one party breaks a contract, the other party has some legal remedies, which means ways to get justice. Read the green box for the first remedy, damages. Damages means money paid as compensation for the loss caused by the breach. But only for loss that arises in the usual course of things, not for remote or indirect loss, unless both parties knew at the time that such loss was likely."),

("Contract Act: More Remedies and Offshoring",
"Here are more remedies under the Contract Act. On the left is a stipulated penalty. Stipulated means agreed in advance. If the contract already names a sum to be paid in case of breach, the injured party gets reasonable compensation up to that amount, and the court may reduce it if it seems too high. In the middle is specific performance. In some special cases, the court orders the defaulting party to actually do what they promised, instead of just paying money. This is given only in exceptional cases. On the right is an injunction, which is a court order to stop doing something. Notice the speed: an injunction can usually be obtained within about a month, while getting damages can take years in our court system. Now read the blue box at the bottom. Foreign companies that offshore work to India protect their data mainly through contracts. They should sign an exhaustive, which means very detailed, Service Level Agreement, or SLA, covering data security and confidentiality."),

("Indian Penal Code: Sections 406 and 420",
"The Indian Penal Code, or IPC, is India's main criminal law. Two sections are relevant to us. On the left is Section 406, punishment for criminal breach of trust. This happens when a person who has been entrusted with property, meaning trusted to look after it, dishonestly misuses it, uses it wrongly, disposes of it, or makes someone else do so. The punishment is imprisonment up to three years, or a fine, or both. On the right is Section 420, about cheating. You may have heard people jokingly call a cheater a four-twenty in India. This is where that comes from. It covers cheating and dishonestly making someone hand over property, or alter or destroy a valuable security. The punishment is imprisonment up to seven years, and a fine. Now read the blue box. Why is this in a security course? Because an employee who misuses data entrusted to them, or a fraudster who tricks people online, can be prosecuted under these sections."),

("Indian Copyright Act: A Timeline",
"Copyright protects creative work, such as books, music, films and software, from being copied without permission. Follow the timeline. In 1957, India passed the Copyright Act. In 1994, it was amended to include satellite broadcasting, computer software and digital technology. So since 1994, software has been clearly protected by copyright in India. In 1999, there was another major amendment, to make the Act fully compatible with TRIPS. Look at the bottom line: TRIPS stands for Trade-Related aspects of Intellectual Property Rights. It is an agreement of the World Trade Organization, or WTO. Also in 1999, the International Copyright Order extended the Act's protection to all WTO member countries. Finally, on the fifteenth of January 2000, the 1999 amendment came into force. Read the note at the bottom. Your study material proudly says that India has one of the most modern copyright protection laws in the world."),

("Copyright Infringement: Section 63B",
"Let us see what happens if someone breaks copyright on software. Read the first point. Any person who knowingly uses an illegal copy of a computer program can be punished under Section 63B. Look at the red box on the left. The punishment is imprisonment for a minimum of seven days, up to three years, and a fine of at least fifty thousand rupees, up to two lakh rupees, or both. Now read the blue box. In India, software has copyright protection, but not patent protection. Why? Because a software program is basically an algorithm, and patent law does not protect algorithms by themselves. Now read the green box. Under Indian law, the word software includes computer programs, databases, computer files, preparatory design material, and printed documentation such as user manuals. So copying a software manual is also covered. The simple lesson for all of you: using pirated software is not just risky because of viruses. It is also a crime."),

("Consumer Protection Act and Specific Relief Act",
"Here are two more laws. On the left is the Consumer Protection Act, 1986. It came into force on the fifteenth of April 1986. Its purpose is to protect consumers from exploitation, and to save them from poor quality goods and deficient services. Look at the shield with a tick. In the context of security, a consumer can complain to the court about deficiency of service. For example, if a company discloses your personal information without proper authorization, that can be treated as a deficiency of service. On the right is the Specific Relief Act, 1963. Look at the balance scale with a red stop sign. According to Section 39 of this Act, a person can claim temporary and permanent injunctions against the unauthorized disclosure of confidential information. An injunction, as we saw, is a court order to stop doing something. So if someone is about to leak your company's secrets, you can ask the court to stop them."),

("Indian Laws for Information Security: Summary",
"This table brings together all the Indian laws we have discussed, and what each one helps with. It is another very good table for your notebook. The IT Act 2000 deals with unauthorized access, viruses, hacking, tampering with source code, and breach of confidentiality. The Indian Contract Act 1872 deals with breach of contract and service level agreements, with remedies like damages, penalties and specific performance. The Indian Penal Code covers criminal breach of trust in Section 406, and cheating in Section 420. The Copyright Act 1957 covers illegal copies of software, in Section 63B. The Consumer Protection Act 1986 covers deficiency of service, such as leaking customer data. And the Specific Relief Act 1963 gives injunctions against disclosure of confidential information, in Section 39. Remember, at the time of your study material, India had no single data protection law, so these six laws together worked as a protective net for information security."),

("Government Initiatives: STQC",
"Now let us look at what the Indian government did to improve security standards. The Ministry of Information Technology started several initiatives. The first is STQC. Look at the diagram on the left: the Ministry of IT, then the Department of Information Technology, or DIT, then the STQC Directorate. Read the blue box. STQC stands for Standardization, Testing and Quality Certification. It was set up because there was international demand that Indian firms should have security standards accredited, or officially approved, at an international level. Read the points. STQC launched an independent third-party certification scheme for Information Security Management Systems, following the standard BS 7799 Part 2. It was accredited by RvA, an accreditation body in the Netherlands. STQC also provides services like testing hardware and software products, certifying products, and training people in quality and security standards. So when a foreign company asks whether an Indian firm is secure, an STQC certificate helps answer that question."),

("CERT-In: Computer Emergency Response Team",
"The second initiative is CERT-In, the Indian Computer Emergency Response Team. It was set up by the Department of Information Technology as part of the international CERT community, to protect India's IT assets against viruses and other security threats. Look at the red circle and its seven functions around it. It serves as a central point for responding to security incidents, with a reliable, trusted contact available twenty-four hours a day. It shares best practices with system administrators and service providers. It raises awareness and understanding of information security among Indian Internet users. It issues advisories and alerts about the latest threats and vulnerabilities. It coordinates between organizations to solve security problems. It builds links with similar organizations around the world. And it does research and development with leading research and educational institutions. You can think of CERT-In as a fire brigade for cyber attacks. CERT-In is still active today, and it publishes security warnings on its website that you can read."),

("ISTDC: Information Security Technology Development Council",
"The third initiative is ISTDC, the Information Security Technology Development Council. Read the blue box on the left. Its main objective is to facilitate, coordinate and promote technological advances in information security, and to respond to security incidents, threats and attacks at the national level. Now look at the four numbered functions on the right. One: evaluate cyber security project proposals and give recommendations to the Department of Information Technology for further processing. Two: review ongoing projects through monitoring committees, and recommend changes in scope, funding, duration, and so on. Three: recommend follow-up action on completed projects, such as transfer of technology or starting the next phase. Four: form review and steering groups for projects approved and funded by the DIT. In simple words, ISTDC is like a guide and supervisor for security research projects in India. It decides which projects are worth funding, and makes sure they are done properly."),

("Legal Issues of Cryptography: Patents",
"Now we come to legal issues in cryptography itself. Read the first point. Your study material, quoting experts, says the legal picture changes quickly, and if you are going to do anything involving cryptography, talk to a lawyer. A patent gives an inventor the exclusive right to use an invention for some years. Read the second point. Most cryptographic techniques were patented, and this slowed down their use. When the Advanced Encryption Standard was chosen, being free of patents was one of the important criteria. Look at the patent certificate on the left. The famous RSA algorithm was developed at MIT. It was patented only in the United States, and its licensing was controlled by one company. The last RSA patent expired on the twentieth of September, 2000. Read the last point. The Digital Signature Standard, or DSS, was advertised as freely licensable, but rival patent claims by Hellman-Merkle and Schnorr made the situation murky, which means unclear, until those patents expired."),

("Important Crypto Patents (All Expired)",
"This timeline shows four important cryptography patents. Luckily, all of them have now expired. First, the Diffie-Hellman patent, number 4,200,770, issued in 1980. It covers the Diffie-Hellman key exchange, which you will study in Module 8. Second, the Hellman-Merkle patent, number 4,218,582, also issued in 1980. It was claimed to cover all public key systems. There was some controversy about whether this was valid. Interestingly, the specific knapsack method described in this patent was later broken. Third, the Rivest-Shamir-Adleman patent, number 4,405,829, issued in 1983. This covers the RSA algorithm, named after its three inventors. Fourth, the Hellman-Pohlig patent, number 4,424,414, issued in 1984, which is related to Diffie-Hellman key exchange. You do not need to memorize the patent numbers, but do remember the names and the years. The main lesson is that, because these patents expired, these powerful algorithms are now free for everyone to use, which helped the growth of Internet security."),

("Export Controls",
"Look at the short poem on the left, written by Ron Rivest, the R in RSA. Mary had a little key, it's all she could export, and all the email that she sent was opened at the Fort. It is a joke about a real problem. Read the points on the right. The US government once placed severe restrictions on exporting encryption. It treated encryption as a dangerous technology, just like germ warfare or nuclear weapons. So American companies could use strong encryption at home, but could export only weak encryption. This caused a lot of bitterness in the computer industry. It also led to some clever technical designs, where domestic products using strong encryption had to work together with weaker exportable products. The Fort in the poem refers to the American intelligence agency, which could read the weakly encrypted emails. Now read the green box. Today, export controls have been largely lifted. But this history shows again how law and technology can clash."),

("Module 5 Summary",
"Let us summarize what we learned in this module. Look at the blue box on the left, Lesson 5, Key Escrow. We saw the dilemma between lawful wiretaps and privacy. We learned how the Clipper and EES system works: each chip has an ID, and its key is split between two escrow agencies. We learned the trustee protocol, with n trustees and a KDC. And we discussed the politics: few advantages for the user, and many disadvantages. Now the green box, Lesson 8, Legal Issues. We learned the four ethical categories, PAPA, what privacy means, and the hierarchy of regulators. We saw how digital signatures are slowly being accepted in law. We studied the IT Act 2000, especially Sections 43, 65, 66 and 72, and other Indian laws: the Contract Act, IPC, Copyright, Consumer Protection and Specific Relief. Finally we saw STQC, CERT-In, ISTDC, patents and export controls. Now look at the red box. There are eight review questions. Please answer all of them before our next class."),

("References",
"Finally, here are the reference books for this module. The first is Cryptography and Network Security by William Stallings, our main textbook. The second is Network Security by Charlie Kaufman and Radia Perlman, which discusses key escrow, patents and export controls in an interesting way. The third is Applied Cryptography by Bruce Schneier, which has a detailed chapter on key escrow and the politics around it. The fourth is Network Security: The Complete Reference by Roberta Bragg. For the Indian laws, I also suggest that you read the actual text of the IT Act 2000, which is freely available on government websites. And here is a small thought exercise for you. Think about the apps on your phone. Which ones know your location, your contacts or your photos? Did you agree to that knowingly? Remember PAPA, and think about your own privacy. Thank you all for your attention. Now, are there any questions? Please feel free to ask."),
]
